# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
# The continuous-retrain workflow's own interpreter, created in the
# runner's workspace - which IS this directory on the self-hosted
# runner. `venv/` above does not cover it (that rule matches a
# directory literally named venv), and clean: false means it persists
# between runs, so without this an interactive `git add .` would sweep
# in thousands of files. See CLAUDE.md section 24.
.ct-venv/

# Data & ML artifacts
data-pipeline/data/raw/*
!data-pipeline/data/raw/.gitkeep
data-pipeline/data/processed/*
!data-pipeline/data/processed/.gitkeep
# games_final.csv is not disposable intermediate output any more: the
# inference service reads it at startup and inference-service/Dockerfile
# copies it into the image, so a fresh clone cannot build without it. Same
# reasoning as ml-training/models/*.json below. Everything else here
# (games_master, games_with_rolling, games_with_features, model_dataset)
# is still regenerable pipeline output and stays ignored.
!data-pipeline/data/processed/games_final.csv
# THE SAME RECLASSIFICATION, TWICE MORE. Both of these are read at startup
# by the inference service and copied by its Dockerfile, so they are
# runtime dependencies rather than regenerable output - exactly what
# games_final.csv turned out to be, and what ml-training/models/*.json
# turned out to be before that. See the standing rule in CLAUDE.md section
# 4: a file added to a Dockerfile COPY list must have its git-tracking
# status checked in the same change, because a green local build proves
# nothing about a fresh clone.
#
# player_boxscores_with_rolling.csv is 77 MB and goes through Git LFS (see
# .gitattributes). It is listed here as a normal exception; LFS intercepts
# it transparently on `git add`. CI needs `lfs: true` on actions/checkout
# or it receives a pointer file instead of the data.
# team_advanced_rolling.csv is the FOURTH instance of the same
# reclassification. Nothing reads its columns for a prediction - they are
# rejected features sitting in UNUSED_FEATURE_COLUMNS - but
# build_final_dataset.py reads the FILE, so a fresh clone cannot build the
# dataset without it. Rebuilding it needs 13,199 gitignored raw box scores,
# which is hours of ingestion; committing 3.2 MB is the cheaper truth.
!data-pipeline/data/processed/team_advanced_rolling.csv
!data-pipeline/data/processed/quarter_half_raw.csv
!data-pipeline/data/processed/player_boxscores_with_rolling.csv
ml-training/models/*
!ml-training/models/.gitkeep
# The 7 production models are a runtime dependency, not build output: the
# inference service refuses to boot unless models/ matches MODEL_REGISTRY
# exactly, and its Dockerfile copies them in. This blanket rule predates
# finalize_models.py, so when it started writing .json files here they
# landed inside an ignore rule already covering the folder and were never
# committed - invisible locally, because docker build reads the filesystem
# and does not care what git tracks. actions/checkout is the first thing
# that only ever sees committed files, which is what surfaced it.
!ml-training/models/*.json
ml-training/mlruns/*
!ml-training/mlruns/.gitkeep

# Retrain output, and it was NEVER actually ignored until 2026-10-07 - CLAUDE.md
# section 3 claimed it was for a month while `git check-ignore` matched no rule
# here at all. It stayed invisible because a promotion has never happened, so the
# directory has never existed; the workflow also names every path it commits
# explicitly, so these could not have been swept in by CI. What it would have
# cost is 8 untracked files in `git status` after any `--force` run, in a repo
# whose own rule is to stage by name precisely because CLAUDE.md must not be
# swept in. Promotion is a pull request against models/, never a copy out of here.
ml-training/models_candidate/

# Java
backend/target/
*.class

# Node
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/build/

# Env & secrets
.env
*.pem

# IDE
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json

# WNBA pipeline data, ignored the same way the NBA's raw data is. The
# processed tables are disposable output, with one exception added in phase 4
# below. Everything else here is regenerable by rerunning the pipeline.
data-pipeline/data/wnba/raw/*
data-pipeline/data/wnba/processed/*

# ...except the one the inference image copies, which stopped being disposable
# output the moment phase 4 began serving from it - the same reclassification
# games_final.csv went through. Caught by `git check-ignore -v` before the
# COPY line was written rather than by a red CI run, which is the whole point
# of the standing rule in CLAUDE.md s4. Everything else under processed/ stays
# ignored because nothing reads it at runtime.
!data-pipeline/data/wnba/processed/wnba_games_final.csv

# The NBA regression gate's baseline is a per-change artifact, not a tracked
# one. Committing it would BREAK the tool: the capture path refuses to
# overwrite an existing baseline, deliberately, so that a baseline taken
# after a change can never hide the regression it was meant to catch - and a
# committed one means the next person to use the gate gets refused. It also
# carries point-in-time values (the fixture count) that rot as a season is
# played. The script is tracked; the snapshot is captured fresh each time.
ml-training/wnba/nba_gate_baseline.json
# The same for each later phase's own baseline. Each phase captures its own so
# an earlier phase's record is never overwritten, and all of them carry
# point-in-time values (a fixture count, a days-behind) that rot as a season
# is played. The gate SCRIPT is tracked; its snapshots are not.
ml-training/gleague/gate_baseline_phase4.json
ml-training/gate_baseline_a4.json

# G League. Third league. The raw per-season pulls stay disposable; three
# processed tables became RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES in phase 4 and are un-ignored
# in the same change that added them to the inference image's COPY list -
# the standing rule in CLAUDE.md section 4, whose count this makes five.
#
# The Showcase Cup table is the one that looks optional and is not: REST_DAYS
# is computed across the Cup boundary, so without it every team's first
# regular-season game of a Cup season would be served a value the models
# never saw for that row - one game per team per season, silently.
data-pipeline/data/gleague/raw/*
data-pipeline/data/gleague/processed/*
!data-pipeline/data/gleague/processed/gleague_games_final.csv
!data-pipeline/data/gleague/processed/gleague_showcase_games.csv
!data-pipeline/data/gleague/processed/gleague_franchise_identity.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NFL (added 2026-10-08, phase 1). EVERY NFL table stays out of the repository,
# raw and processed alike - no exceptions, unlike the three leagues above.
#
# The reason is the licence, not size: the source is English Wikipedia, whose
# content is CC BY-SA 4.0. Share-alike on a derived table committed to a public
# repository is a decision the owner has taken deliberately in the other
# direction - the data lives on disk and, later, on the served volume only, and
# attribution appears on the NFL pages in phase 5. See data-pipeline/nfl/
# DATA_LICENSE.md, which is committed and describes what these files are.
#
# So there is no un-ignoring exception here. If a future phase needs an NFL
# table at runtime it arrives on the volume, never through git.
data-pipeline/nfl/data/
